  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat comes not from one breakthrough but from two compounding curves: endurance (how long a single agent can work autonomously, per METR's time-horizon data) and breadth (how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate, illustrated by Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign run through Claude Code). Its central claim is that the real danger is economic: attackers who steal cloud access, as in Sysdig's \"LLMjacking,\" get capability for free while the victim pays the bill, so price and usage gates that filter buyers fail against tenants with stolen keys. The piece also uses probabilistic reasoning (1 − (1 − p)^n) to show that cheap, parallel attempts make a defender's per-attempt success rate insufficient. The tone is a pointed, opinionated analysis, written for security engineers, platform teams, and skeptics who dismiss agentic threats as hype, and it relies on a recurring framing device drawn from *The Terminator*: Kyle Reese's \"absolutely will not stop\" line, reinterpreted as endless billing and retry loops rather than a murderous robot. Terms to keep consistent in translation include \"endurance,\" \"breadth,\" \"orchestrator,\" \"sub-agents,\" \"LLMjacking,\" and \"the meter.\"",
